(vector (map (:adr/id 2790003353), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-3353 — Government Social Benefits Officials Documentation and Logistics-Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3353)), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000 2607152500)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-3353), (:actor/name "Government Social Benefits Documentation and Logistics-Coordination Practice"), (:actor/domain :public-administration/social-benefits-documentation), (:actor/isco-08 "3353"), (:actor/wave 4), (:actor/robotics? true), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-application-record :schedule-caseworker-appointment :flag-eligibility-review :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-eligibility-review)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :applicant-verified :office-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion)), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "approve the benefit claim" "approved the benefit claim" "deny the benefit claim" "denied the benefit claim" "reject the benefit claim" "rejected the benefit claim" "terminate the existing benefit" "terminated the existing benefit" "confirm the benefit approval" "confirmed the benefit approval" "confirm the benefit denial" "confirmed the benefit denial" "execute the benefit denial" "executed the benefit denial" "execute the benefit termination" "executed the benefit termination" "finalize the eligibility decision" "finalized the eligibility decision" "issue the final eligibility determination" "issued the final eligibility determination" "cut off the existing benefit" "cut off the applicant's existing benefit" "給付を承認した" "給付の承認を確定した" "給付を却下した" "給付の却下を確定した" "給付を打ち切った" "受給資格を確定した")), (:phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-application-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-application-record :schedule-caseworker-appointment :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-application-record :schedule-caseworker-appointment :coordinate-supply-order :flag-eligibility-review}, :auto #{:log-application-record :schedule-caseworker-appointment :coordinate-supply-order}}}"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3353"), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-3353"), (:test/counts "{:total 25, :assertions 60, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :applicant-verification :office-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:test/simulator :socialbenefits.actor-test), (:db/id -1), (:adr/body "# ADR 2790003353: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 3353 Government Social Benefits Officials documentation/logistics-coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise — a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 3353 -- Government Social\nBenefits Officials** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"3353\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"3353\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present), and its `:name` read exactly \"Government\nSocial Benefits Officials\" -- confirming the premise. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3353` (`gh api`\n404-confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry\nedit promote the `3353` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This occupation directly controls people's livelihoods.** Government\nSocial Benefits Officials determine eligibility for and approve/deny\nsocial benefits (welfare, unemployment, disability, etc.) -- decisions\nwith direct, severe impact on vulnerable people's basic subsistence.\nThis is the same class of risk the fleet already encountered in the\nperson-facing-service track: **ADR-2607152500 (Wave4 rollout start\namendment)** established that decisions directly touching a person's\nsafety, dignity or subsistence must always exclude the closed op\nallowlist and always escalate to a human -- not merely be gated behind\na confidence threshold. This ADR applies that same guardrail to a\nbenefits-eligibility context: the guardrail here is even stricter than\na typical always-escalate op, because the eligibility-determination\ncapability itself is never given an op identity at all (see Decision).\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals) and, for the closed-allowlist +\ncontent-based scope-exclusion shape specifically,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3333`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3333)\n(Employment Agents and Contractors) -- both independently re-cloned and\nre-tested in this session, adapted substantially to the vulnerable-\npopulation guidance above rather than following either reference's\ndomain shape directly.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3353` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for government social\nbenefits officials, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as\nevery prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Case\nDocumentation Advisor ⊣ SocialBenefits Governor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- this is a different\nlibrary coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor, human-in-\nthe-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit\nledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **documentation and logistics coordination\nONLY, never eligibility-determination authority** -- it must NEVER be\ndesigned to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY\nbenefits-eligibility-determination authority:\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-application-record` -- benefit-application intake/status data\n  entry (documentation only, not a determination)\n- `:schedule-caseworker-appointment` -- caseworker meeting/review\n  appointment scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-eligibility-review` -- surface an application that needs\n  human caseworker eligibility review (new application, status-change\n  trigger, appeal), ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER auto-commit-\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- office-equipment procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**:\napproving a benefit, denying a benefit, or terminating an existing\nbenefit. These are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op such as\n`:approve-benefit-claim` cannot even be proposed by the reference mock\nadvisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of the four allowlist\nops), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a\nmisbehaving fork) ever produced such an op, the governor's closed-\nallowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`) rejects it unconditionally and\npermanently -- verified by dedicated tests\n(`hard-on-op-not-allowed`/`-deny`/`-terminate` in\n`socialbenefits.governor-test`).\n\n### Six HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Applicant unverified** -- any proposal referencing an\n   `:applicant-id` must resolve to an independently\n   `:registered?`/`:verified?` applicant record. Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim.\n2. **Office unverified** -- same, for any proposal referencing an\n   `:office-id` (office-equipment supply-order coordination).\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in an `:approve-benefit-claim`/`:deny-benefit-claim`/\n   `:terminate-benefit`-shaped op.\n5. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check, any\n   proposal whose `:rationale` names a finalization/execution action\n   for a benefit approval, denial or termination (e.g. \"approved the\n   benefit claim\", \"terminated the existing benefit\") is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content, not just on\n   the `:op` field. This is defense-in-depth on top of #4: even if a\n   future change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection\n   still independently blocks eligibility-determination language.\n6. **Official/office record must be independently verified/registered\n   before any action** -- folded into checks #1/#2 above; no proposal\n   commits or escalates on an unverified applicant or office record.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the\ngeneral confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-eligibility-review` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless\n  of confidence or phase (independently agreed by both the governor's\n  `always-escalate-ops` and the phase table's `:auto` set, which never\n  contains this op at any phase 0-3). This is the ONLY channel by which\n  the robot may surface an observation suggesting an eligibility\n  decision is needed -- it never resolves the observation itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:estimated-cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence.\n- (LLM confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for any\n  op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the\nsibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"benefit\", \"eligibility\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal, self-\nblocking the happy path -- this is exactly the risk this vertical's own\nname (\"Social Benefits Officials\") and its own vocabulary (every\nproposal necessarily discusses \"benefit\"/\"eligibility\" concepts to do\nits documentation job) could trigger if phrased carelessly. This\nactor's `socialbenefits.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"approved the\nbenefit claim\", \"terminated the existing benefit\"), never bare nouns,\nand `socialbenefits.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-\nself-trip-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting\nevery op in the closed allowlist, driven through the default mock\nadvisor, never trips `:scope-excluded` (or any other HARD violation).\nThe mock advisor's own default rationale template (\"documented <op>\nfor applicant <id>[ and office <id>]\") was written to avoid any bare-\nnoun collision by construction. Green on first run in this session --\nno self-trip was actually hit during development, but this ADR still\ndocuments the guard and its regression test since the risk class is\nespecially acute for this domain's vocabulary.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"3353\" ...}` entry is\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3353` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3353`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented count\n  bumped accordingly (recomputed from the live registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not by `grep -c`, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 25 tests, 60 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors this batch (`cloud-itonami-isco-3313`/`-3333`\n  do NOT depend on `kotoba-lang/technology`; only the central\n  `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry itself depends on it, to resolve\n  `:required-technologies` stacks).\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to tx-data\") --\n  a single-entity EDN vector with a `:adr/body` string blob rather than\n  a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- This ADR's structural-exclusion design (Decision section) is intended\n  as the reference pattern for any future ISCO/ISIC actor whose\n  occupation directly gates a vulnerable population's safety, dignity or\n  subsistence -- the same shape as ADR-2607152500's guardrail, restated\n  here with a concrete worked example (closed allowlist + content-based\n  scope exclusion + single always-escalate flag-op) for the next agent\n  to mirror.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an `:approve-benefit-claim`/`:deny-benefit-claim` op\n  that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops` (the same shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the explicit task guidance and the\n  ADR-2607152500 precedent -- an always-escalate op still means the\n  *capability* to approve/deny/terminate a benefit exists in the code\n  path and is merely deferred to a human gate; for a decision this\n  severe, the capability itself must not exist. The only op related to\n  eligibility is `:flag-eligibility-review`, which surfaces the need for\n  a decision to a human and never itself decides anything, approves\n  anything, or defers an approval/denial action for later automatic\n  execution.\n- A numeric ceiling check on benefit amounts (mirroring 3313's\n  transaction-amount ceiling): rejected as the primary guardrail -- a\n  ceiling implies the actor is authorized to approve amounts up to some\n  threshold, which is exactly the authority this actor must not have.\n  A numeric threshold is used ONLY for the unrelated, genuinely low-\n  stakes `:coordinate-supply-order` op (office-equipment procurement),\n  where an amount ceiling is an appropriate, proportionate control.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"benefit\" \"eligibility\"\n  \"approve\" \"deny\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip\n  bug class described above -- this actor's own domain necessarily\n  discusses benefit/eligibility concepts to do its documentation job\n  (e.g. `:flag-eligibility-review`'s purpose IS to flag an eligibility\n  concern), so a bare-noun list would have self-blocked the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals.\n- Modeling `office` as an unverified free-text field instead of a\n  provenance-checked entity (mirroring only `applicant` verification):\n  rejected -- office-equipment supply-order coordination against an\n  unverified office would let a proposal route procurement through an\n  unregistered location, so `office` gets the same independent\n  registered+verified HARD check as `applicant`, matching the two-\n  entity provenance shape already established by `cloud-itonami-isco-\n  3333`'s candidate+employer pattern.")))
